<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Frum movement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1930s–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The John Frum movement is a religious movement on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanna">Tanna</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a>, often described as a cargo cult. It arose in the late 1930s around a figure called John Frum, who promised the islanders wealth if they abandoned missionary rule and returned to custom. The arrival of large American forces in the New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> strengthened the belief, and followers still hold an annual celebration on 15 February.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-frum-movement.jpg" alt="John Frum movement" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Flickr user Charmaine Tham · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Frum_gathering_area.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The movement drew on existing religious practice around Sulphur Bay on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanna">Tanna</a>, above all the worship of Keraperamun, a god associated with Mount Tukosmera; a claim made in 1949 placed its beginnings as early as the 1910s. Accounts of the figure differ. In one he was first known as John Broom, who would return from a distant land to sweep away the White colonials. In another a local man named Manehivi took the alias John Frum and appeared in a Western-style coat, promising houses, clothes, food and transport.</p><p>Others hold that John Frum was a spirit vision induced by kava and a manifestation of Keraperamun. His followers were to reject money, Western education, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and work on the copra plantations and return to kastom, the Bislama word for custom. In 1941 they spent their money, left the missionary churches, schools, villages and plantations and moved inland for traditional feasts, dances and rituals. Most had come from the Presbyterian church.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The colonial authorities tried to suppress the movement, arresting a Tannese man who called himself John Frum, humiliating him in public, imprisoning him and exiling him and other leaders to another island. During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> 50,000 American troops were stationed in the islands with vast supplies, and about 10,000 Ni-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a> men served in the Vanuatu Labor Corps of the United States Armed Forces during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalcanal-campaign">Guadalcanal campaign</a>. Their experience gave the faith the character of a cargo cult, and after the war followers built symbolic landing strips to draw American aeroplanes bringing cargo.</p><p>In 1957 the leaders formed the Tanna Army, a non-violent body that paraded with imitation American caps, singlets lettered T-A USA and bamboo guns. When the government moved against it, some members barricaded themselves in their headquarters with real guns, and the leaders were tried and jailed in Vila. In 1959 a follower told David Attenborough that if others could wait two thousand years for Jesus Christ, he could wait more than nineteen years for John.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the late 1970s followers opposed the creation of a united, independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanuatu">Vanuatu</a>, fearing that a central government would favour Western modernity and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> over local custom. The movement reportedly had over 5,000 members in the 1990s and fewer than 500 by 2022. Europeans who claimed to be the leader of the prophecy, such as Claude-Philippe Berger, who styled himself traditional king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanna">Tanna</a>, gained status on the island by promising development and investment.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Frum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the Second World War Espiritu Santo became a huge American base, and the <b>John Frum movement</b> spread on Tanna.</blockquote><a href="/state/new-hebrides-condominium">New Hebrides Condominium</a></li></ul></section>
